# {{ model.productName }} {{ model.productVersion }} download:
{{ model.productName }} is a compiled variant of Saxon from the Java
platform to the C/C++ platform using GraalVM `native-image`.

{{ model.productName }} provides processing in XSLT 3.0, XQuery 3.1 and XPath 3.1, and,
in SaxonC-EE, Schema validation 1.0/1.1.

## What’s included in this download?
In this download, the binary shared libraries (.{{ model.soExtension }} files) for the
core part of Saxon, compiled with `native-image`, which we call 
`{{ model.libsaxoncCore }}`, along with the sources for the C/C++-specific API,
which we call `{{ model.libsaxonc }}`, and its binary shared libraries, along with
the {{ model.binariesSentence }} binaries for running XSLT and XQuery from the
command line.

{% include model.isUnix ? "install_unix.md.peb" : "install_windows.md.peb" %}

## Running the binaries
Once you have the binaries on your path, you should be able to run the binaries
directly, for example:

	Transform -s:input.xml -xsl:stylesheet.xsl

{% include "bin_doc_links.md.peb" %}

## Integrating with CMake-using project

If you're planning to use both the provided `{{ model.libsaxoncCore }}` and
`{{ model.libsaxonc }}` `.{{ model.soExtension }}` files, then the `samples`
directory provides a fully working example, which you can boil down to the
following snippet for a `CMakeLists.txt`:

    list(APPEND CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH "{{ model.cmakeCompatInstallPath }}")
    find_package({{ model.SaxonCPackage }} {{ model.productVersion }} EXACT REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(YourTarget PRIVATE {{ model.SaxonCPackage }}::{{ model.SaxonCTarget }})

(Where `{{ model.installPrefix }}` is where you installed the libraries.)

If you're planning to compile `{{ model.libsaxonc }}` yourself, to use the C++
API, then you can effectively use CMake’s `FetchContent` in your
`CMakeLists.txt` to integrate it into your project:

    include(FetchContent)
    FetchContent_Declare(
        {{ model.SaxonCPackage }}

        URL {{ model.cmakeCompatInstallPath }}

        SOURCE_SUBDIR src
    )
    FetchContent_MakeAvailable({{ model.SaxonCPackage }})

That will automatically use the `{{ model.SaxonCCorePackage }}` bundled as
part of the `.zip` (which we assumed you downloaded to
`{{ model.installPrefix }}` in the example). If you need to use different
SaxonCCore, for example when you're cross-compiling, then you need to specify
where CMake can find that version, and then prevent it from using the bundled
version:

    # Assume we are compiling on a Linux x86_64 host and have downloaded an Arm64
    # version of SaxonCCore to /opt/saxonica/{{ model.SaxonCPackage }}-linux-arm64-{{ model.productVersion | replace({'.': '-'}) }},
    # and x86_64 version to /opt/saxonica/{{ model.SaxonCPackage }}-linux-x86_64-{{ model.productVersion | replace({'.': '-'}) }}

    include(FetchContent)
    FetchContent_Declare(
        SaxonCHE
        SaxonCHE-macos-arm64-12-9-0
        URL /opt/saxonica/{{ model.SaxonCPackage }}-linux-x86_64-{{ model.productVersion | replace({'.': '-'}) }}

        SOURCE_SUBDIR src
    )

    if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR STREQUALS "aarch64")
        set(NO_BUNDLED_SAXONC_CORE TRUE)
        list(APPEND CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH /opt/saxonica/{{ model.SaxonCPackage }}-linux-arm64-{{ model.productVersion | replace({'.': '-'}) }})
    endif()
    FetchContent_MakeAvailable({{ model.SaxonCPackage }})


## Other resources
SaxonC-specific samples can be found at the [saxonc-resources][] GitHub
repository.

[saxonc-resources]: https://github.com/Saxonica/saxonc-resources

Full documentation for SaxonC 12 is available. The [SaxonC 12  documentation][]
contains information specific to SaxonC - including instructions for installing
and configuring the product, details of the SaxonC C/C++ API, PHP API and
Python3 API, and sample code. Meanwhile, the main [Saxon 12 documentation][]
contains further information relevant to SaxonC.

[SaxonC 12 documentation]: https://www.saxonica.com/saxon-c/documentation12/index.html

[Saxon 12 documentation]: https://www.saxonica.com/documentation12/index.html

